          <h2>What I learned</h2>

          <p>
            The most valuable part of this project was really familiarizing myself with AWS services
            and the production migration process. It was a chance to practice the full end-to-end
            workflow of taking a local containerized app and making it run safely and reliably in the cloud.
            The steps I took can be summarized as follows:
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>Start with a local containerized app.</li>
            <li>Replace dev-only runtime assumptions.</li>
            <li>Externalize the database.</li>
            <li>Move secrets into managed storage.</li>
            <li>Put the container behind a real load balancer.</li>
            <li>Add CI/CD with immutable image tags.</li>
            <li>Use short-lived cloud credentials instead of static keys.</li>
            <li>Codify the infrastructure.</li>
            <li>Validate teardown and rebuild.</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            It was the end-to-end nature of the project that made it so valuable.
            I could have easily spun up an ECS Fargate service with a public container
            and called it a day. But that would have missed the point of the project,
            which was to practice the full production migration process and all the
            operational considerations that come with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            This project gave me hands-on practice across Flask, Docker,
            MongoDB, ECS Fargate, ECR, ALB, SSM Parameter Store, CloudWatch,
            GitHub Actions, OIDC, IAM, and AWS CDK. More importantly,
            it forced me to think like a production engineer:
            security boundaries, release traceability, cost control, rollback
            paths, and infrastructure ownership.
          </p>

